Chlorofier
Ingredients: DUBSTEP + RAW, GRITTY & BRUTALIST + Keyboards
Creative Catalyst: its founding artists were botanists who sonified the electrical signals of rainforest plants.

Chlorofier sounds like a rusted concrete greenhouse collapsing into a peat bog during a category-five storm. Subverting the pristine digital sheen usually associated with electronic music, it strips bass music down to unpolished, acoustic-sounding earth and exposes the skeletal, industrial architecture of raw brute-force sound design. Instead of glossy plastic synthesizers, the genre forces heavy, detuned keyboards through rotting analog preamps that hum with organic feedback. The signature low-end does not merely shake a club; it mimics the tectonic, subterranean grinding of shifting root systems and snapping taproots, punctured by jagged, unpredictable metallic percussion.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 140 BPM, lurching and uneven, swinging like a heavy iron gate hanging from a single rusted hinge.
- Harmonic Palette: Dissonant microtonal shifts, harsh minor thirds, and moist, detuned keyboard clusters that sound like keys swelling up from humidity.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Xylem-Gating: Using live bio-impedance signals from weeping rubber trees to trigger sidechain compression, causing the music to gasp and choke unpredictably.
  2. Concrete Sub-Saturator: Running digital sub-bass through a crushed, brutalist cement-mixer impulse response to add jagged, porous gravel textures to the low end.
  3. Overdriven Key-Bed Slap: Amplifying the acoustic clatter of plastic and wooden keyboard keys striking their keybeds louder than the notes themselves.
- Dynamics: A track does not drop; it rots inward. It starts as a claustrophobic hum of damp soil and slowly metastasizes into a monolithic block of concrete-cracking sonic pressure before collapsing abruptly into silence.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in the humid, abandoned brutalist botanical research domes of the Daintree rainforest in late 2021, Chlorofier was pioneered by a renegade collective of disgraced agronomists who wired copper biosensors straight into the vascular bundles of carnivorous pitcher plants. Spurned by the pristine commercialism of modern electronic music, they threw illegal raves inside concrete half-pipes reclaimed by strangler figs, wearing garments fashioned from rotting Hessian sacks, woven vines, and heavy welded-rebar jewelry. Their rituals involved blindfolded listening sessions where participants had to stand barefoot in deep mud, letting the sub-bass frequencies rattle their vertebrae while the botanical synths wailed with the real-time electrical agony of over-watered ferns.

Central Instruments: Overdriven vintage electric pianos treated like percussion instruments, modified Roland Jupiter synths patched directly into damp soil, rusted spring-reverb plates, and brutalist frequency-modulation bass cannons.

